---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

const h2 = 'text-terminal-amber text-base font-semibold mt-10 mb-2';
const link = 'text-terminal-cyan hover:text-terminal-amber transition-colors hover:underline break-words';
const p = 'text-terminal-text text-sm mb-3';
const code = 'text-terminal-amber';
---

<Layout
  title="My Little Library: a web app built to be shipped — Benoît Guelin"
  description="Media library (films, series, books): tracking, collections, ratings. A personal project run like a product."
  lang="en"
>
  <main class="py-24">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6">
      <a href="/" class="inline-block mb-8 text-sm text-terminal-text-dim hover:text-terminal-amber transition-colors">
        <span class="text-terminal-green">$</span> cd ~/portfolio
      </a>

      <div class="mb-6 text-terminal-text text-sm">
        <span class="text-terminal-green">$</span> <span class="text-terminal-amber">cat</span> <span class="text-terminal-cyan">./my-little-library.md</span>
      </div>

      <article class="border border-terminal-green/30 p-6 bg-terminal-bg-light">
        <h1 class="text-terminal-amber-bright text-xl font-semibold mb-6">My Little Library: a web app built to be shipped</h1>

        <p class={p}>
          Media library (films, series, books): tracking, collections, ratings. Closed beta, waiting list open: <a href="https://www.mylittlelibrary.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={link}>https://www.mylittlelibrary.io/ <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span><span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>
        </p>

        <p class={p}>
          A personal project run like a product: what matters to me is not only that the app runs, but that it can be changed without breaking anything and shipped without manual steps.
        </p>

        <section>
          <h2 class={h2}>A design system in its own package</h2>
          <p class={p}>
            The interface lives in a separate package that the app consumes: twenty-five components (buttons, modals, media cards, fields, status badges…), design tokens, a Storybook, and unit tests on the components that carry behaviour.
          </p>
          <p class={p}>
            Notable choice: moving the interface out of the app lets it be built and tested in isolation, and enforces a single palette on every screen.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class={h2}>A CI that blocks before a human reads</h2>
          <p class={p}>
            Every pull request goes through a "Quality" job (lint, TypeScript check, in-house checks on the code and on the deployment scripts), a dependency security audit, then the tests (Japa on the server, Playwright end to end).
          </p>
          <p class={p}>
            Notable choice: whatever a machine can verify never comes back to human review.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class={h2}>Delivery without intervention</h2>
          <p class={p}>
            Merging a "Release PR" cuts a version. The server image is built, started and checked on every pull request, published on every version, then deployed on its own to a VPS behind Traefik. A <code class={code}>/health</code> route reports the running version, and a rollback means re-running the delivery with an older tag.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class={h2}>Stack</h2>
          <p class={p}>
            AdonisJS 6, strict TypeScript, PostgreSQL, React 19 with Inertia, Tailwind, pnpm monorepo, Docker.
          </p>
        </section>
      </article>
    </div>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
